<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#pathDomain> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The specific domain of this path, which may differ from the parent entity's general domain. Example: Oshun Ibu Kole governs vultures and transformation, distinct from Oshun's general river domain. Access: public-unrestricted."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#SpiritKinshipRelationScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Relations de parenté spirituelle"@fr .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#pathName> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The name of this specific path or camino. Example: Oya Yansa, Oya Igbale, Oshun Ibu Kole, Obatala Ocha Grinan. Access: public-unrestricted."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko-nkisi> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#imports> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko-core> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#entityType-ajogun> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Ajogun are not evil spirits in a moral sense but cosmic forces of adversity that must be balanced. Their propitiation is a legitimate archival and ritual category distinct from orisa worship."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#entityType-espiritu-commission> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#path> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Links a SpiritualEntity to its known Path instances (caminos/avatars). A single entity may have many paths. Access: public-unrestricted."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#commissionType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#commission-marinero> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#CommissionTypeScheme> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#offeringDetailed> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#SpiritualEntity> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#entityType-orisa> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#EntityTypeScheme> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#path> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#salutation> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#minimumAccessLevel> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#access-initiated-only> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#entityType-ori> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The individual divine head and personal destiny in Yoruba and Yoruba-derived traditions. Not an external spirit but the innermost self and personal divine principle. Ori has a shrine (ile ori), receives rogacion and propitiation, and is considered the most important force in an individual's life. Its guidance supersedes that of the orisa."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#polarity-androgynous> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Andrógino"@pt .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#entityType-lwa-ghede> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Spirits of the Ghede nanchon in Haitian Vodou. Govern death, sexuality, resurrection, and the boundary between living and dead. Liminal in temperament. Composite New World origins distinct from both Rada and Petwo."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#PolarityModalityScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#hasTopConcept> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#polarity-temporal> .
